[Thinking]
**Creating a Bug Bounty Program Project**

I'm thinking about developing a project that focuses on searching for bug bounty programs that offer compensation. This could be set up as a web app with a backend that scrapes data from sources like HackerOne and Bugcrowd. I’ll need to outline the file structure, include sample code using Node.js, Express, and React, and list necessary dependencies. I also want to clarify user preferences, like hosting and API keys, to cover any missing details. Let’s build a comprehensive plan!
[/Thinking]

Below is a complete project blueprint for a tool that searches for bug bounty programs offering monetary compensation. I provide:

- project scope and feature list
- recommended architecture
- file/folder structure and list of files
- dependencies/libraries
- code for a minimal but functional prototype (backend, frontend, scraper, DB schema, tests)
- Docker and deployment basics
- detailed comments and improvement ideas
- follow-up questions so we can make this fully functional for your needs

High-level summary
- A full-stack web app: Node.js/Express backend + Postgres (or SQLite/Mongo) + simple scraper/aggregator + React frontend.
- The app ingests bounty-program data from public APIs (HackerOne, Bugcrowd if you have API keys), RSS feeds, and targeted scraping (with care for robots.txt and rate limits), normalizes data, stores it, and provides search/filter endpoints and a web UI.
- Authentication (optional), scheduled ingestion, and user-saved searches/alerts.

Core features (MVP)
- Ingest bounty programs from:
  - HackerOne / Bugcrowd APIs (if API keys provided)
  - Public program directories (e.g., GitHub issues pages, third-party lists)
  - RSS feeds or CSV exports if available
  - Manual input via admin UI
- Normalize key fields: name, scope (in-scope, out-of-scope), reward range, platforms, tags (XSS, RCE), URL, program status, contact, last updated
- Search by keyword, tags, reward range, platform, status
- Sort/filter results
- Pagination
- Admin endpoints to re-run ingestion or add manual entries
- Basic frontend to display results and filters
- Scheduler (cron) to re-run ingestion periodically

Suggested architecture
- Backend: Node.js + Express (or NestJS if you prefer structure)
- Scraper/ingestor: Node.js workers using axios + cheerio for scraping; use APIs when available
- Database: Postgres (recommended) or SQLite for quick local testing; MongoDB is an alternative
- Frontend: React (Create React App or Vite) with simple component-based UI
- Optional: Redis for caching and rate-limiting
- Containerization: Docker + docker-compose
- CI: GitHub Actions for tests and lint
- Hosting: Heroku, Render, Vercel (frontend), AWS/GCP for production

File structure (recommended)
- bounty-search/
  - README.md
  - package.json
  - docker-compose.yml
  - Dockerfile
  - .env.example
  - backend/
    - package.json
    - src/
      - index.js
      - app.js
      - config/
        - db.js
        - logger.js
      - routes/
        - programs.js
        - admin.js
      - controllers/
        - programsController.js
        - adminController.js
      - services/
        - ingestionService.js
        - hackeroneService.js
        - bugcrowdService.js
        - scraperService.js
      - models/
        - programModel.js
        - index.js
      - jobs/
        - ingestJob.js
      - utils/
        - normalize.js
        - validators.js
      - tests/
        - programs.test.js
    - Dockerfile
  - frontend/
    - package.json
    - src/
      - index.jsx
      - App.jsx
      - api/
        - api.js
      - components/
        - SearchBar.jsx
        - ProgramList.jsx
        - ProgramCard.jsx
        - Filters.jsx
      - pages/
        - Home.jsx
      - styles/
        - App.css
  - infra/
    - nginx.conf (optional)
    - postgres-init.sql (optional)
  - scripts/
    - ingest-local.sh

Dependencies and libraries

Backend (Node/Express)
- express
- axios (HTTP requests, APIs)
- cheerio (HTML parsing for scraping)
- node-cron or bull (for scheduling)
- pg / sequelize or knex (for Postgres ORM)
- sqlite3 (if using SQLite locally)
- dotenv (env vars)
- cors
- winston (logging)
- joi (validation)
- helmet (security)
- rate-limiter-flexible (rate limiting)
- jest + supertest (tests)

Frontend (React)
- react, react-dom
- axios (API calls)
- react-router (if multi-page)
- tailwindcss or bootstrap (optional)
- react-query (optional, for caching)

Docker
- official node image
- official postgres image

Complete initial code (minimal prototype)
- I’ll provide a minimal working backend (Express + SQLite) and simple React frontend that queries backend. This prototype will work locally without API keys. It will include a basic scraper that loads a local sample dataset and supports search/filter.

Note: For production scraping of sites like HackerOne/Bugcrowd, use their APIs and follow terms and rate limits. Scraping those sites may violate terms; prefer official APIs.

